WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.212
Graph2Image starts with a biological network. Edges encode relationships, and every node carries a vector of measured attributes.

2
00:00:09.212 --> 00:00:15.866
It partitions the nodes into communities, which summarize network connectivity before spatial mapping.

3
00:00:15.866 --> 00:00:24.566
Distances among community centroids are arranged on a two-dimensional grid using optimal transport, producing the structural channel.

4
00:00:24.566 --> 00:00:35.826
Separately, correlations among node attributes define a second optimal-transport layout. The structural and feature layouts are size-matched and stacked as distinct channels.

5
00:00:35.826 --> 00:00:45.550
A convolutional neural network is trained on one multi-channel image per node and returns class probabilities for held-out nodes.

6
00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:58.857
After model fitting, SHAP is computed as a separate explanation step. Feature-channel pixel attributions are mapped back to the original biological attributes and summarized by class.

7
00:00:58.857 --> 00:01:22.399
After image construction, classifier memory no longer grows with the retained edge count. On Pan-Cancer, Graph2Image used all sixty-nine million edges; message-passing baselines exceeded twenty-five gigabytes and were down-sampled to three point two million edges. This is a memory and analyzable-scale result, not a training-speed claim.

8
00:01:22.399 --> 00:01:51.060
In five-fold stratified cross-validation, Graph2Image reached eighty-two point nine percent accuracy and sixty-eight point six percent macro F one on DepMap genes, and ninety-three point two percent accuracy and eighty-six percent macro F one on T C G A breast cancer. Separately, co-complex gene pairs were enriched thirty-one point two fold among the DepMap network edges.
